# Stripe refund enforcement point (development deployment)

Generated by `auths init stripe-refund-approval`. An AI agent may refund a
Stripe payment only when the managers named below approve that exact refund,
and only inside the limits below. The Stripe key lives only in the Auths
gateway, which decides every refund. An auditor checks every refund
afterwards, offline.

**This is a development deployment, not an isolated one:**

- one OS user, a file attempt store, and a software observer key;
- development custody: the root, the managers, and the agent sign with key
  files on this host;
- it cannot establish credential isolation: every role runs as the same OS
  user, so any process of that user can read every key here, the gateway's
  credential store included.

Nothing here claims credential isolation. That needs separate OS users and
`auths-gateway doctor`, which this project does not set up.

## Roles

| Role | Its files | Its commands |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Operator | `operator/` (root key, trust, grant), `enforcement/gateway/` (the gateway's state and the Stripe key) | `bin/setup`, `bin/install-gateway`, `bin/start`, `bin/stop` |
| Agent | `agent/` (its key and grant), `requests/` | `bin/request`, `bin/submit`, `bin/export` |
| Manager | `managers/<name>/` (one key each) | `bin/approve` |
| Auditor | `audit/pins.json`, `audit/bundle.json` | `bin/audit` |

Each role's key is in its own 0700 directory. Besides the project's public
files (`enforcement.toml` and `recipe/`), the agent's client reads only
`agent/`, `operator/setup.json` (public facts), and `requests/`; it never
reads the root's or a manager's key. On one OS user this is a code
convention, not isolation.

The gateway's admin socket is `run/admin.sock`, not the gateway's default
inside its state directory: give `--admin-socket` with that path to any
admin command you run yourself, such as `auths-gateway status`.

## Parameters

@@PARAMETERS@@

`enforcement.toml` records them with this directory, the Python interpreter
and `auths` command every wrapper runs, and the gateway binary with its
sha256. Every command refuses to run if the project was moved or copied, or
if the gateway binary changed (`auths.gateway.changed`): run `auths init`
again instead.

## Commands, in order

Every command prints one JSON object on standard output. It exits 0 on
success or on a normal refusal, and non-zero with a stable code otherwise.

```sh
cd @@DIR@@
bin/setup
bin/install-gateway < KEY_FILE
bin/start
bin/request --operation-id refund-1 --payment-intent PI --amount 1500 --approvers @@APPROVERS@@
bin/approve @@FIRST_MANAGER@@ requests/refund-1/@@FIRST_MANAGER@@.request   # each listed manager
bin/submit --operation-id refund-1
bin/export
bin/audit
bin/stop
```

**`bin/setup`** (operator) creates the root, agent, and manager keys, the
gateway's trusted context, and the agent's grant carrying the limits above.
It runs `auths-gateway review` and `bound-extension`, which contact nothing.
It refuses a project that is already set up.

**`bin/install-gateway < KEY_FILE`** (operator) installs the gateway with
the Stripe key on standard input, then creates its observer key and
completes `audit/pins.json`. The key reaches only the gateway, which stores
it under `enforcement/gateway/`; no file, argument, environment variable, or
log here receives it, and a terminal on standard input is refused. The
recipe accepts only a Stripe restricted test key, whose prefix is
`rk_test_`; the gateway refuses any other before it stores anything
(`gateway.install.credential-guard`). With the Stripe double (the default),
the wrapper reads the key into memory to give the double the sha256 of the
bearer token it must expect, less one trailing newline, and writes only that
digest. In Stripe test mode it never reads its standard input: the install
inherits it. A second install is refused (`gateway.install.already-installed`).

With the double, the key can be any made-up value with the `rk_test_` prefix:
the double checks only the gateway's bearer token against the digest the
install recorded. Keep `KEY_FILE` outside this directory and readable only by
you, for example:

```sh
( umask 077; printf 'rk_test_%s\n' "$(od -An -tx1 -N12 /dev/urandom | tr -d ' \n')" > ../refunds.key )
bin/install-gateway < ../refunds.key
```

**`bin/start`** and **`bin/stop`** (operator) start and stop the gateway, and
in double mode the counting Stripe double on 127.0.0.1. The gateway listens
on `run/app.sock` for the agent and on `run/admin.sock` for the operator.

**`bin/request`**, **`bin/approve`**, **`bin/submit`** (agent and managers).
The agent writes one approval request per listed manager into
`requests/<operation-id>/` and approves its own. Each manager answers with
`bin/approve NAME REQUEST`, which shows the review and asks before signing;
`--decline` signs a decline. `bin/submit` collects the responses and sends
every assembled proof to the gateway, which alone decides the approval
threshold, the ceiling, the per-window count and sum, and whether an
operation ID may run again. Every record says who decided it in
`decided_by`: `gateway`, `approver`, or `client`. `request --precheck` is an
opt-in client-side pre-check, not an enforcement boundary.

**`bin/export`** (agent) writes `audit/bundle.json`: one entry per operation
ID, the approval responses, the recipe, and the trusted context.

**`bin/audit`** (auditor) runs `auths-gateway audit` offline on the bundle
with the two pinned values in `audit/pins.json`, and passes its report,
standard error, and exit status through unchanged. By default the audit
fails the bundle when any entry has no gateway-signed outcome
(`audit.unverified`), as for the refusals the gateway made before it
recorded anything. `bin/audit --allow-unverified-refusals` passes such an
entry only when the audit itself refuses its proof; an entry without an
outcome whose proof verifies still fails. That option has a limit: an entry
whose outcome was removed and whose proof was then altered also passes, as
`unverified` with `admitted: false`, just as an entry removed from the
bundle would. The audit checks the entries in the bundle; it cannot show
that none were left out.

**`bin/selftest`** (anyone), in double mode, right after `bin/start` on a
fresh project: it makes its own refunds, so it refuses a project that has
already made one (`auths.selftest.not-fresh`), and it uses up the agent's
refunds for the current window. Try your own refunds in a second project.

```sh
AUTHS_SELFTEST_KEY_FILE=KEY_FILE bin/selftest
```

It sends hostile refunds through a witness, a relay on the application
socket that runs as its own process, and requires the gateway to decide
each one; a request refused only by `request --precheck` must fail that
check. It then exports, audits with and without
`--allow-unverified-refusals`, checks that eight tampered copies of the
bundle fail and that the known limit above behaves as stated, checks that a
second install is refused, and scans this project, `$TMPDIR`, and `/tmp` for
the key outside `enforcement/gateway/`. It reads the key file only for that
scan, and writes `audit/selftest.json`. It shows what the gateway decides
against a counting double on one host. It does not show credential
isolation, and the double is not Stripe.

## Stripe test mode

Generate the project with `--provider stripe-test`, a gateway built without
`--features loopback-provider`, your platform account (`--platform-account`),
and the connected account the grant lists (`--connect-account`). Pipe your
own restricted test key into `bin/install-gateway`. `bin/selftest` runs only
against the double.
